@anon.0de5ee8f4a9046f07397637c35d34c13.2126 = private unnamed_addr constant [710 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for membership tests on `list` and `tuple` literals.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AWhen testing for membership in a static sequence, prefer a `set` literal\0Aover a `list` or `tuple`, as Python optimizes `set` membership tests.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0A1 in [1, 2, 3]\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0A1 in {1, 2, 3}\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0AThis rule's fix is marked as unsafe, as the use of a `set` literal will\0Aerror at runtime if the sequence contains unhashable elements (like lists\0Aor dictionaries). While Ruff will attempt to infer the hashability of the\0Aelements, it may not always be able to do so.\0A\0A## References\0A- [What\E2\80\99s New In Python 3.2](https://docs.python.org/3/whatsnew/3.2.html#optimizations)\0A", align 1
