@anon.0de5ee8f4a9046f07397637c35d34c13.2154 = private unnamed_addr constant [2466 x i8] c"## What it does\0A\0AChecks for use of standalone type variables and parameter specifications in generic functions.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0A\0ASpecial type parameter syntax was introduced in Python 3.12 by [PEP 695] for defining generic\0Afunctions. This syntax is easier to read and provides cleaner support for generics.\0A\0A## Known problems\0A\0AThe rule currently skips generic functions nested inside of other functions or classes and those\0Awith type parameters containing the `default` argument introduced in [PEP 696] and implemented\0Ain Python 3.13.\0A\0ANot all type checkers fully support PEP 695 yet, so even valid fixes suggested by this rule may\0Acause type checking to fail.\0A\0A## Fix safety\0A\0AThis fix is marked unsafe, as [PEP 695] uses inferred variance for type parameters, instead of\0Athe `covariant` and `contravariant` keywords used by `TypeVar` variables. As such, replacing a\0A`TypeVar` variable with an inline type parameter may change its variance.\0A\0AAdditionally, if the rule cannot determine whether a parameter annotation corresponds to a type\0Avariable (e.g. for a type imported from another module), it will not add the type to the generic\0Atype parameter list. This causes the function to have a mix of old-style type variables and\0Anew-style generic type parameters, which will be rejected by type checkers.\0A\0A## Example\0A\0A```python\0Afrom typing import TypeVar\0A\0AT = TypeVar(\22T\22)\0A\0A\0Adef generic_function(var: T) -> T:\0A    return var\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A\0A```python\0Adef generic_function[T](var: T) -> T:\0A    return var\0A```\0A\0A## See also\0A\0AThis rule replaces standalone type variables in function signatures but doesn't remove\0Athe corresponding type variables even if they are unused after the fix. See\0A[`unused-private-type-var`][PYI018] for a rule to clean up unused\0Aprivate type variables.\0A\0AThis rule will not rename private type variables to remove leading underscores, even though the\0Anew type parameters are restricted in scope to their associated function. See\0A[`private-type-parameter`][UP049] for a rule to update these names.\0A\0AThis rule only applies to generic functions and does not include generic classes. See\0A[`non-pep695-generic-class`][UP046] for the class version.\0A\0A[PEP 695]: https://peps.python.org/pep-0695/\0A[PEP 696]: https://peps.python.org/pep-0696/\0A[PYI018]: https://docs.astral.sh/ruff/rules/unused-private-type-var/\0A[UP046]: https://docs.astral.sh/ruff/rules/non-pep695-generic-class/\0A[UP049]: https://docs.astral.sh/ruff/rules/private-type-parameter/\0A", align 1
