@anon.0de5ee8f4a9046f07397637c35d34c13.2229 = private unnamed_addr constant [1005 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for use of `zip()` to iterate over successive pairs of elements.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AWhen iterating over successive pairs of elements, prefer\0A`itertools.pairwise()` over `zip()`.\0A\0A`itertools.pairwise()` is more readable and conveys the intent of the code\0Amore clearly.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aletters = \22ABCD\22\0Azip(letters, letters[1:])  # (\22A\22, \22B\22), (\22B\22, \22C\22), (\22C\22, \22D\22)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Afrom itertools import pairwise\0A\0Aletters = \22ABCD\22\0Apairwise(letters)  # (\22A\22, \22B\22), (\22B\22, \22C\22), (\22C\22, \22D\22)\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0A\0AThe fix is always marked unsafe because it assumes that slicing an object\0A(e.g., `obj[1:]`) produces a value with the same type and iteration behavior\0Aas the original object, which is not guaranteed for user-defined types that\0Aoverride `__getitem__` without properly handling slices. Moreover, the fix\0Acould delete comments.\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `itertools.pairwise`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/itertools.html#itertools.pairwise)\0A", align 1
